WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
I don't agree with that.

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:04.800
Then why the fuck did you accept the position!

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.180
Who the fuck hired you?!

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:08.420
You should step down!

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.860
If that is what you think about  being on [unintelligible] you should step down!

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.040
That 2015 confrontation on the Yale campus

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:18.520
between Nicholas Christakis, a sociologist and physician,

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:19.960
and protesting students

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.320
was part of a controversy over how to dress up on Halloween.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.080
It all started a week earlier when the school's Intercultural Affairs Council

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.300
sent an email encouraging members of the community to be careful

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:35.740
not to offend their fellow students with culturally and racially insensitive costumes.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.160
Christakis' wife Erika, an expert in early childhood education at the university

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.160
responded with her own thoughts.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.260
'Is there no room anymore for a child or young person to be a little bit obnoxious...

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:49.800
a little bit inappropriate or provacative or, yes, offensive?'

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.960
'American universities' she wrote 'have become places of censure and prohibition.'

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.140
Students said that by sending her email

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:59.920
Christakis had failed to create a safe space at Yale's Sillman College,

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.920
where she served as associate master.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.920
Nicolas Christakis jumped into the fray,

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.040
defending his wife’s email, and then he tried to engage in a dialogue with protestors.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.880
Scenes of students shouting at Christakis, one of Yale’s most respected faculty members, went viral.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.260
Do not interrupt me, I was not angry

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.600
and now I want your job to be taken from you.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.100
I don't want you to have this job, I am disgusted knowing that you work at Yale University

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.960
where I will get my degree.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.120
Where I will look back and think I have to argue with you.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.440
Don't, no, sir, sir sir don't do it, don't do it-

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.460
Christakis not only held onto his tenured professorship

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:41.000
but three years later he was awarded the Sterling Professorship, Yale’s highest faculty honor.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.620
And his confrontation with students kicked off an ongoing national debate about freedom of speech,

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.440
political correctness, and Millennial sensitivity on college campuses.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.020
Homophily or the fact that we prefer to connect with people we resemble

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.880
have fundamental significance.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.680
As a sociologist, the 56-year-old Christakis is no stranger to highly charged group interactions.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.800
His new book is Blueprint: The Evolutionary Origins of a Good Society,

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.380
which argues that our genetic makeup predisposes us to favor  peaceful interaction

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:15.040
and respectful co-existence over angry and violent mob rules.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.560
I have a vision of us as people that actually privileges our common humanity

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.420
that is interested not in what is different among us, but what is the same.

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.200
I sat down with Christakis to talk about his theory

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.260
that what unites us as humans is stronger than what divides us,

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:33.400
the power of evolution as an explanatory system for society

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.400
and whether enlightenment values

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.200
such as civil discourse and intellectual freedom are still respected in our nation's colleges and universities.

